1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,132
Welcome to the Seal Hall,
also known as the Seal Beach
or the Hall of Two Beaches.
This is the final section
of the tourist route through
the Grotta del Bue Marino.
The name comes from the

2
00:00:13,132 --> 00:00:25,495
presence of sand: as you can
see, we are no longer
walking on an artificial
walkway but on the cave's
actual floor. The hall
is dedicated to Monachus
monachus, the Mediterranean

3
00:00:25,495 --> 00:00:37,789
monk seal. It was right
here, in the 1970s, that one
of the last specimens still
present in the Gulf
of Orosei was observed:
a mother with her pup. These
small internal beaches

4
00:00:37,789 --> 00:00:50,362
offered an ideal refuge:
a sheltered, silent place,
safe from storm swells and
— in former times — from
human presence as well. They
were perfect spaces for
resting and nursing the

5
00:00:50,362 --> 00:01:03,075
young. This is not simply
a hall of the cave: it is
a place that preserves one
of the last testimonies
of the monk seal's presence
in these waters. Among all
the halls on the tourist

6
00:01:03,075 --> 00:01:15,508
route of the Grotta del Bue
Marino, this is the one that
changes most from year
to year, precisely because
of the active presence
of the river. The sand
beneath our feet does not

7
00:01:15,508 --> 00:01:27,592
come from the sea, but from
the underground river.
During winter, depending
on the amount of rainfall,
the river can increase its
flow considerably.
At certain times it even

8
00:01:27,592 --> 00:01:39,536
covers much of the floor,
bringing in new sand
or carrying some away, and
slightly altering the
morphology of the space.
What for us marks the end
of the tourist route is,

9
00:01:39,536 --> 00:01:50,852
in reality, also the
beginning of the
speleological section of the
system. From this point
onwards, the cave continues
and branches out for
kilometres, connecting

10
00:01:50,852 --> 00:02:03,076
to the Grotta di Su Palu,
the Grotta di Su Molente,
and the Grotta di Monte
Longos, forming the
approximately 75 kilometres
of explored passages known
today. The speleological

11
00:02:03,076 --> 00:02:15,998
section consists of large
halls rich in fresh water,
wide internal beaches,
imposing formations, and
numerous sumps: environments
completely filled with water
that can only be navigated

12
00:02:15,998 --> 00:02:27,803
by experienced cavers using
scuba equipment and tanks.
The visit ends here. Before
beginning the return
journey, you will
be directed according
to your next destination:

13
00:02:27,803 --> 00:02:40,027
Cala Luna, the mini-cruise,
or the port of Cala Gonone.
Each group will have
a specific exit order, so
it is essential that you
listen carefully to the
guides' instructions so

14
00:02:40,027 --> 00:02:52,670
as not to risk missing your
boat. Thank you for choosing
to visit the Grotta del Bue
Marino. We hope this journey
has moved you and allowed
you to discover something
more about this

15
00:02:52,670 --> 00:02:57,000
extraordinary territory and
the underground world
it conceals.
